# Nasyad scope

## Product boundaries

- **Local-first only** — no cloud sync, network APIs, or accounts unless product scope explicitly expands.
- Data stays on device; export/import is the backup path.
- Supported locales: `en`, `fa`. Calendar system is independent of language.

## Engineering boundaries

- Active stack only — see `pubspec.yaml` and `.cursor/skills/nasyad-flutter/stack.md`.
- **Prefer packages over rewrites** — for general-purpose behavior (parsing, formatting, algorithms, platform bridges, reusable UI), use a maintained `pub.dev` package instead of hand-rolling it. Reuse packages already in `pubspec.yaml` first; add a new one when no active dependency fits.
- **When to write custom code** — Nasyad-specific domain logic, thin wrappers around existing stack, or no suitable package (maintenance, license, size, or product boundary — e.g. cloud sync while scope is local-first).
- **Adding a dependency** — pick a well-maintained package with reasonable transitive weight; update `docs/AGENTS.md` for structural or gated additions (`get_it`, `injectable`, `freezed`, network clients, and other stack-shaping deps).
- Do not add `get_it`, `injectable`, `freezed`, or network clients without updating `docs/AGENTS.md`.
- Do not create aspirational folders (`injection/`, `maintenance/`) until they exist in code and docs.
- Never edit generated `*.g.dart` files.

## Agent boundaries

- Do not invent capabilities outside [`.cursor/skills/fdm-roster/roster.md`](../skills/fdm-roster/roster.md) — offer to add a skill/rule instead.
- Missing roster asset: skip phase with note; do not invent inline guidance.

## Authority

- Layout and layers: `docs/AGENTS.md`
- User-visible structure: `.cursor/skills/nasyad-product/app-map.md`
- UX persona: `.cursor/skills/nasyad-ux/end-user.md`

When docs disagree on UX after a ship, **app-map wins**; sync `docs/app-description.md` if acceptance criteria changed.
